# facts
fact("ecology","bears","In spring bears return to the meadows; a family of bears (carle, quean and cubs) seen; bears fierce and proud in the wastes and intolerant of other creatures, but they avoided two armed men in sunlight.",[r("BTHREE-C13-P002","although they were fierce and proud in those wastes, and could not away with creatures that were not of their kind")])
fact("weapons","Ralph","Ralph slays the bear with a spear thrust to the side, cuts off a fore-paw, and drives his sword over the shoulder to the heart.",[r("BTHREE-C13-P003","drave the sword in over the shoulder so happily that it reached his heart")])
fact("household","Ralph and Ursula","They cook meat (venison) at a fire on the toft before the cave; Ursula 'dights victual'; Ralph skins and breaks up the bear and hangs skin and carcase on a tree.",[r("BTHREE-C14-P003","there was already venison roasting at the fire on the toft"),r("BTHREE-C13-P022","Then he hung the skin and the carcase on a tree of the grove")])
fact("ecology","Valley of Sweet-chestnuts","Spring in the valley: birdsong, scent of blossoms on the west wind.",[r("BTHREE-C13-P007","the scent of many blossoms went past on the wings of the west wind")])
fact("custom","marriage","Ursula recalls that since heathen days weddings were witnessed by the whole folk; the couple wait for the Innocent Folk to be witnesses besides God.",[r("BTHREE-C13-P012","while our people were yet heathen, when a man should wed a maid all the folk knew of it")],context="dialogue:Ursula")
fact("magic","Well at the World's End","Ursula holds that the perils of the homeward road will overcome them unless they have drunk of the Well.",[r("BTHREE-C13-P018","the perils of the homeward road shall overcome us, despite of love")],context="dialogue:Ursula",conf="medium")
fact("custom","Innocent Folk","The messengers come every spring to the cave; it is many a year since they last found seekers there; the elder once led a horse-riding knight to the Well in his youth.",[r("BTHREE-C14-P009","it is many and many a year ago since we found any seekers after the Well dwelling here"),r("BTHREE-C15-P002","led a man to the Well who was riding a horse and was clad in knightly array")],context="dialogue:the elder")
fact("transport","Innocent Folk","The Innocent Folk had never seen horses (the young men), riding oxen and using oxen as pack beasts; the sheep-folk also marvelled at horses and weapons.",[r("BTHREE-C15-P002","Their horses and all their gear were a great wonder to the young men, for they had seen no such beasts before"),r("BTHREE-C15-P003","they marvelled exceedingly at their horses and weapons")])
fact("weapons","Innocent Folk","Messengers bear no armour; green-stone bladed axes; young men carry strong bows and quivers.",[r("BTHREE-C14-P002","bore no armour, but each had an axe with a green stone blade")])
fact("food","Innocent Folk","The messengers bring raiment, meal and wine on sumpter-beasts; wine in well-hooped kegs, carved wooden cups; the elder bakes cakes from meal at the fire.",[r("BTHREE-C14-P011","there was wine there in well-hooped kegs, and wooden cups fairly carven"),r("BTHREE-C14-P011","had taken meal thence, and was making cakes at the fire")])
fact("custom","Innocent Folk","For their own weddings the Innocent Folk build a bower in which the couple spend the wedding night.",[r("BTHREE-C14-P009","such a bower as we build for our own folk on the night of the wedding")],context="dialogue:the elder")
fact("architecture","bridal bower","Built of cut willow boughs, driven stakes and woven twigs (wattle), roofed with skins, garlanded, floor strewn with flowers.",[r("BTHREE-C14-P010","cut willow boughs, and such-like wood, and drave stakes and wove the twigs together")])
fact("clothing","Innocent Folk","Wedding raiment of fine white wool embroidered with feathers of bright-hued birds.",[r("BTHREE-C14-P011","raiment of fine white wool for those twain, broidered in strange but beauteous fashion")])
fact("custom","marriage","The wedding is made by the couple standing before the elder at moonlit night after a feast and bidding the witnesses bear witness; no priest.",[r("BTHREE-C14-P012","they stood before the elder, and bade him and the young men bear witness that they were wedded")])
fact("religion","Ralph","Ralph asks whether wedding and carnal knowledge would hurt the Quest; the elder says they hear not that it shall be worse.",[r("BTHREE-C14-P009","we hear not that it shall be the worse for you in any wise that ye shall become one flesh")],context="dialogue:the elder")
fact("agriculture","Innocent Folk","Their land has sheep and neat in plenty, garths of young corn, and vineyards on hillsides; cots scattered in the valley; sheep described as uncouth-looking.",[r("BTHREE-C15-P003","they passed by garths wherein the young corn was waxing, and vineyards on the hillsides")])
fact("society","Innocent Folk","The folk are kind but over-reverent, greeting with more worship than fellowship; no filth, famine, poverty or misery; town unfenced for war.",[r("BTHREE-C15-P003","These men greeted them kindly, and yet with more worship than fellowship"),r("BTHREE-C15-P004","Yet was there naught of filth or famine, nor any poverty or misery")])
fact("custom","Innocent Folk","Runners go ahead to announce arrivals; townsfolk line the way with bowed heads; seekers are bathed, clad in fine fragrant white gowns, crowned with flowers and set on a dais like kings or gods; feast with songs by youths and maidens.",[r("BTHREE-C15-P004","for runners had gone before them"),r("BTHREE-C15-P006","they set them down on a dais as though they had been kings, or rather gods")])
fact("religion","Innocent Folk","A single great house serves as Temple, Mote-house and Guest-house.",[r("BTHREE-C15-P005","the Temple, or Mote-house, or Guest-house, for it was all these")])
fact("custom","Innocent Folk","Their songs are simple and harmless, about the fairness of the earth and the happy loves of creatures.",[r("BTHREE-C15-P007","concerning the fairness of the earth and the happy loves of the creatures that dwell therein")])
